alex_m: Heads-up — I'm about to push a Terraform change to migrate the gpu-nodepool from p4d to a mixed set (a40 + g5) with capacity reservations for the A40s. Want to run this by infra first because there are a couple of tricky pieces: spot fallback, taints/tolerations, and secret wiring for instance profiles. Plan excerpt below. :eyes:

alex_m: ```hcl
resource "aws_instance" "gpu_a40" {
  instance_type = "g4dn.12xlarge" # A40 equivalent
  capacity_reservation_specification {
    capacity_reservation_preference = "open"
    capacity_reservation_target { capacity_reservation_id = var.a40_reserve_id }
  }
  lifecycle { create_before_destroy = true }
}

module "gpu_pool" {
  source = "org/gpu-nodepool/aws"
  instance_types = ["g4dn.12xlarge", "g5.12xlarge"]
  spot_percent = 60
  taints = { gpu = "true:NoSchedule" }
}
```

alex_m: Questions: 1) Will the capacity reservation target trick us during rolling update? 2) If a spot falls back, we want to prefer g5 on-demand rather than a smaller instance; how to wire that in TF? 3) Any gotchas for kube labels/taints so runtime doesn't schedule non-inference pods?

tara_ops: Nice, thanks for the snippet. Quick answers: 1) create_before_destroy helps but capacity reservation binding can cause AWS to hold onto instances if the reservation ID is strict — make sure we only attach target when the reservation exists. Maybe use a conditional var.enable_reservation. 2) For spot fallback ordering, I'd prefer separate nodegroups: one reserved A40 group, one spot mixed-gpu group with fallback policy to on-demand g5. That makes lifecycle & scaling clearer.

samir_infra: Agree with tara. Also for TF: don't hardcode reservation_id in the instance resource; use aws_ec2_capacity_reservation data source + count to avoid plan surprises. For ordering prefer: reserved nodegroup (tainted), spot nodegroup (tainted same), then on-demand fallback nodegroup (no taint or a different taint?).

jordan_sre: On the Kubernetes side: ensure daemonsets/infra pods use nodeAffinity to avoid landing on GPU nodes. Use nodeSelector + tolerations only for workloads that actually need GPUs. Also add a global admission annotation to block default scheduling e.g., kube-system -> tolerations: none.

alex_m: Good call. For the taints I was thinking: taint: inference=true:NoSchedule and then any pod with toleration inference=true get scheduled. Is that the right pattern?

tara_ops: Yes. Make sure the autoscaler config includes --node-group-auto-discovery tags so it recognizes the separate groups. Also update cluster-autoscaler expander to prefer larger instances for fallback, otherwise it might pick small on-demand by default.

samir_infra: Secret wiring note: our instance profile for g5 on-demand needs k8s-iam role to mount S3 caches for model shards. If you create a new nodegroup, attach the existing role via launch template. Don't commit IAM ARNs inline in TF; reference from SSM or data aws_iam_role. And add a tf plan guard to ensure role_arn != null.

deploy-bot: deploy:terraform-plan for branch alex/gpu-mix completed — 7 resources to add, 3 to change. See: https://ci.redwood.ai/plan/4589 :package:

alex_m: Thanks. I'll split into three TF modules (reserved-a40, spot-gpu, fallback-g5) and open the PR shortly. Can folks review the IAM/taint bits when it's up?

tara_ops: Happy to. Ping me in PR and @jordan_sre for autoscaler config. :thumbsup:

jordan_sre: I'll check the CA flags (`--expander=least-waste` vs `random`) and the drain hooks for GPU pods. Remember to add preStop to model-server containers so KV caches flush before termination.

alex_m: Will do. I'll aim to open PR in the next hour. If no objections by EOD I'll merge with canary=5%.
